WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.169 --> 00:00:04.710
Dear Fellow Scholars, this is Two Minute Papers
with Dr. Károly Zsolnai-Fehér.

00:00:04.710 --> 00:00:12.220
Virtual Reality, VR in short, is maturing
at a rapid pace and its promise is truly incredible.

00:00:12.220 --> 00:00:17.690
If one day it comes to fruition, doctors could
be trained to perform surgery in a virtual

00:00:17.690 --> 00:00:23.410
environment, we could train pilots with better
flight simulators, teach astronauts to deal

00:00:23.410 --> 00:00:26.740
with zero-gravity simulations, you name it.

00:00:26.740 --> 00:00:31.620
And you will see that with today’s paper,
we are able to visit nearly any place from

00:00:31.620 --> 00:00:32.620
afar.

00:00:32.620 --> 00:00:38.569
Now, to be able to do anything in a virtual
world, we have to put on a head-mounted display

00:00:38.569 --> 00:00:43.870
that can tell the orientation of our head,
and often, hands at all times.

00:00:43.870 --> 00:00:46.039
So, what can we do with this?

00:00:46.039 --> 00:00:48.059
Oh boy, a great deal.

00:00:48.059 --> 00:00:54.489
For instance, we can type on a virtual keyboard,
or implement all kinds of virtual user interfaces

00:00:54.489 --> 00:00:56.709
that we can interact with.

00:00:56.709 --> 00:01:02.229
We can also organize imaginary boxes, and
of course, we can’t leave out the Two Minute

00:01:02.229 --> 00:01:09.320
Papers favorite, going into a physics simulation
and playing with it with our own hands.

00:01:09.320 --> 00:01:14.090
In this previous work, hand-hand interactions
did not work too well, which was addressed

00:01:14.090 --> 00:01:19.159
one more paper down the line, which absolutely
nailed the solution.

00:01:19.159 --> 00:01:24.219
This followup work would look at our hands
in challenging hand-hand interactions, and

00:01:24.219 --> 00:01:30.090
could deal with deformations, lots of self-contact
and self-occlusion.

00:01:30.090 --> 00:01:32.289
Take a look at this footage.

00:01:32.289 --> 00:01:38.509
And, look, interestingly, they also recorded
the real hand model with gloves on.

00:01:38.509 --> 00:01:42.520
We might think, what a curious design decision!

00:01:42.520 --> 00:01:44.060
What could that be for?

00:01:44.060 --> 00:01:49.719
Well, what you see here is not a pair of gloves,
what you see here is the reconstruction of

00:01:49.719 --> 00:01:52.640
the hand model by this followup paper.

00:01:52.640 --> 00:01:54.479
Absolute witchcraft.

00:01:54.479 --> 00:02:01.640
Now, as you see, we can manipulate objects,
or even wash our hands in virtual reality.

00:02:01.640 --> 00:02:06.850
This is all great when we play in a computer
game, because the entire world around us was

00:02:06.850 --> 00:02:12.739
previously modeled, so we can look and go
anywhere, anytime.

00:02:12.739 --> 00:02:15.840
But what about operating in the real world?

00:02:15.840 --> 00:02:19.390
What if we wish to look at a historic landmark
from afar?

00:02:19.390 --> 00:02:24.700
Well, in this case, someone needs to capture
a 360-degree photo of it.

00:02:24.700 --> 00:02:28.709
A regular photo will not cut it, because we
can’t turn our head around and look behind

00:02:28.709 --> 00:02:29.730
things.

00:02:29.730 --> 00:02:32.269
And this, is what today’s paper will be
about.

00:02:32.269 --> 00:02:39.739
This new paper is called Omniphotos, and it
helps us produce this 360 view synthesis,

00:02:39.739 --> 00:02:44.739
and when we put on that head-mounted display,
we can really get a good feel of a remote

00:02:44.739 --> 00:02:48.840
place, a group photo, or an important family
festivity.

00:02:48.840 --> 00:02:55.010
So, clearly, the value proposition is excellent,
but we have two questions.

00:02:55.010 --> 00:02:58.280
One, what do we have to do for it?

00:02:58.280 --> 00:02:59.280
Flailing.

00:02:59.280 --> 00:03:00.280
Yes.

00:03:00.280 --> 00:03:02.490
We need to be flailing.

00:03:02.490 --> 00:03:08.840
You see, we need to attach a consumer 360-camera
to a selfie stick, and start flailing for

00:03:08.840 --> 00:03:10.630
about 10 seconds.

00:03:10.630 --> 00:03:11.630
Like this.

00:03:11.630 --> 00:03:19.160
This is a crazy idea, because now, we created
a ton of raw data, roughly what you see here.

00:03:19.160 --> 00:03:25.239
So this, is a deluge of information, and the
algorithm needs to crystallize all this mess

00:03:25.239 --> 00:03:28.230
into a proper 360 photograph.

00:03:28.230 --> 00:03:35.069
What is even more difficult here is that this
flailing will almost never create a perfect

00:03:35.069 --> 00:03:41.049
circle trajectory, so the algorithm first
has to estimate the exact camera positions

00:03:41.049 --> 00:03:43.360
and view directions.

00:03:43.360 --> 00:03:49.159
And hold on to your papers, because the entirety
of this work is handcrafted, no machine learning

00:03:49.159 --> 00:03:52.879
is in sight, and the result is quite general
technique,

00:03:52.879 --> 00:03:58.730
or in other words, it works on a wide variety
of real-world scenes, you see a good selection

00:03:58.730 --> 00:04:00.640
of those here.

00:04:00.640 --> 00:04:02.030
Excellent.

00:04:02.030 --> 00:04:07.579
Our second question is, this is, of course,
not the first method published in this area,

00:04:07.579 --> 00:04:10.099
so how does it relate to previous techniques?

00:04:10.099 --> 00:04:12.410
Is it really better?

00:04:12.410 --> 00:04:15.319
Well, let’s see for ourselves!

00:04:15.319 --> 00:04:21.290
Previous methods either suffered from not
allowing too much motion, or, the ones that

00:04:21.290 --> 00:04:26.660
give us more freedom to move around, did it
by introducing quite a bit of warping into

00:04:26.660 --> 00:04:28.370
the outputs.

00:04:28.370 --> 00:04:32.070
And now, let’s see if the new method improves
upon that.

00:04:32.070 --> 00:04:34.870
Oh yeah, a great deal!

00:04:34.870 --> 00:04:40.760
Look, we have the advantages of both methods,
we can move around freely, and additionally

00:04:40.760 --> 00:04:43.840
there is much less warping than here.

00:04:43.840 --> 00:04:49.540
Now, of course, not even this new technique
is perfect, if you look behind the building,

00:04:49.540 --> 00:04:55.100
you see that the warping hasn’t been completely
eliminated, but it is a big step ahead of

00:04:55.100 --> 00:04:56.320
the previous paper.

00:04:56.320 --> 00:05:00.020
While we look at some more side by side comparisons.

00:05:00.020 --> 00:05:03.630
One more bonus question: what about memory
consumption?

00:05:03.630 --> 00:05:07.680
Well, it eats over a gigabyte of memory.

00:05:07.680 --> 00:05:12.450
That is typically not too much of a problem
for desktop computers, but we might need a

00:05:12.450 --> 00:05:17.410
little optimization if we wish to do these
computations on a mobile device.

00:05:17.410 --> 00:05:19.420
And now comes the best part.

00:05:19.420 --> 00:05:24.250
You can browse these Omniphotos online through
the link in the video description, and even

00:05:24.250 --> 00:05:30.780
the source code, and a Windows-based demo
is available that works with and without a

00:05:30.780 --> 00:05:31.900
VR headset.

00:05:31.900 --> 00:05:35.100
Try it out and let me know in the comments
how it went!

00:05:35.100 --> 00:05:41.430
So, with that, we can create these beautiful
Omniphotos cheaply and efficiently, and navigate

00:05:41.430 --> 00:05:45.170
the real world as if it were a computer game.

00:05:45.170 --> 00:06:27.490
What a time to be alive!

00:06:27.490 --> 00:06:36.550
Thanks for watching and for your generous
support, and I'll see you next time!

